(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-appendicular-skeleton-quiz-version-3"]={"title":"The Appendicular Skeleton — Anatomy Exam 4 — Set 3","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How many bones make up the appendicular skeleton in the adult?","choices":["126 bones","80 bones","206 bones","32 bones"],"answer":0,"exp":"The appendicular skeleton contains 126 bones — the limbs plus the pectoral and pelvic girdles. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which two bones form the pectoral (shoulder) girdle on each side?","choices":["The humerus and the scapula","The clavicle and the scapula","The clavicle and the sternum","The scapula and the first rib"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each pectoral girdle is formed by the clavicle anteriorly and the scapula posteriorly, connecting the upper limb to the axial skeleton. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"With which part of the sternum does the sternal end of the clavicle articulate?","choices":["The xiphoid process below","The body of the sternum","The manubrium of the sternum","The jugular notch margin"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial (sternal) end of the clavicle articulates with the manubrium of the sternum at the sternoclavicular joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"What is the general shape of the clavicle when viewed from above?","choices":["A double curve, like a flattened letter S","A single smooth C-shaped arch","A perfectly straight horizontal rod","A sharp right-angled elbow bend"],"answer":0,"exp":"The clavicle has a double curvature, convex anteriorly on its medial portion and concave anteriorly on its lateral portion, giving an S-like shape. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which fossa lies on the anterior (costal) surface of the scapula, facing the ribs?","choices":["The supraspinous fossa","The subscapular fossa","The infraspinous fossa","The glenoid fossa"],"answer":1,"exp":"The subscapular fossa is the broad concavity on the anterior (costal) surface of the scapula that faces the rib cage. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"The supraspinous and infraspinous fossae of the scapula are separated by which structure?","choices":["The coracoid process","The medial border","The spine of the scapula","The glenoid cavity"],"answer":2,"exp":"The scapular spine is the prominent posterior ridge that divides the posterior surface into the supraspinous fossa above and the infraspinous fossa below. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Which scapular structure directly receives the head of the humerus?","choices":["The acromion","The subscapular fossa","The scapular notch","The glenoid cavity"],"answer":3,"exp":"The glenoid cavity (fossa) is the shallow lateral socket of the scapula that articulates with the head of the humerus at the glenohumeral joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The head of the humerus articulates with which structure?","choices":["The glenoid cavity of the scapula","The acromion of the scapula","The radial notch of the ulna","The coracoid process of the scapula"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rounded proximal head of the humerus articulates with the glenoid cavity of the scapula to form the shoulder (glenohumeral) joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which distal humeral structure articulates with the head of the radius?","choices":["The trochlea","The capitulum","The olecranon fossa","The medial epicondyle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The capitulum is the rounded lateral condyle of the distal humerus that articulates with the head of the radius. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The trochlea of the humerus articulates with which bone?","choices":["The radius","The scaphoid","The ulna","The clavicle"],"answer":2,"exp":"The trochlea is the medial, pulley-shaped articular surface of the distal humerus that articulates with the trochlear notch of the ulna. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which posterior depression of the distal humerus receives the olecranon during full extension of the elbow?","choices":["The coronoid fossa","The radial fossa","The intertubercular sulcus","The olecranon fossa"],"answer":3,"exp":"The olecranon fossa is the deep posterior depression of the distal humerus that accommodates the olecranon of the ulna when the elbow is fully extended. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The surgical neck of the humerus is clinically important mainly because it is a common site of what?","choices":["A common fracture site","Origin of the biceps tendon","Articulation with the glenoid","Attachment of a forearm membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"The surgical neck, just distal to the tubercles, is a frequent fracture site of the proximal humerus, which is why it is named 'surgical.' — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"On which side of the forearm is the ulna located in anatomical position?","choices":["The lateral side","The medial side","The anterior side only","The posterior side only"],"answer":1,"exp":"In anatomical position (palm forward), the ulna is the medial forearm bone, lying on the little-finger side. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which anterior projection of the proximal ulna forms the lower part of the trochlear notch?","choices":["The olecranon","The styloid process","The coronoid process","The radial tuberosity"],"answer":2,"exp":"The coronoid process is the anterior projection of the proximal ulna that forms the inferior margin of the trochlear notch and fits into the coronoid fossa in flexion. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which structure connects the shafts of the radius and ulna along their length?","choices":["The anular ligament","The joint capsule of the elbow","The intertubercular sulcus","The interosseous membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"The interosseous membrane is the fibrous sheet that connects the interosseous borders of the radial and ulnar shafts along their length. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"On which side of the forearm is the radius located in anatomical position?","choices":["The lateral (thumb) side","The medial (little-finger) side","The posterior midline","The anterior midline"],"answer":0,"exp":"In anatomical position the radius is the lateral forearm bone, lying on the thumb side. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"With which two carpal bones does the distal end of the radius primarily articulate at the wrist?","choices":["The trapezium and the trapezoid","The scaphoid and the lunate","The capitate and the hamate","The pisiform and the triquetrum"],"answer":1,"exp":"The distal radius articulates chiefly with the scaphoid and lunate of the proximal carpal row at the radiocarpal (wrist) joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"How many carpal bones are in each wrist, and in how many rows are they arranged?","choices":["10 bones in 2 rows","8 bones in a single row","8 bones in 2 rows","5 bones in 1 row"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each wrist contains 8 carpal bones arranged in a proximal row and a distal row of four each. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which carpal bone is the most frequently fractured, typically from a fall on an outstretched hand?","choices":["The pisiform","The trapezoid","The hamate","The scaphoid"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scaphoid, on the lateral (thumb) side of the proximal row, is the most commonly fractured carpal bone, often from a fall on an outstretched hand. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"How many phalanges are in each hand, and how many does the thumb have?","choices":["14 phalanges; the thumb has 2","14 phalanges; the thumb has 3","12 phalanges; the thumb has 2","10 phalanges; the thumb has 1"],"answer":0,"exp":"Each hand has 14 phalanges — three in each finger and two in the thumb (pollex), which lacks a middle phalanx. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The rounded distal head of a metacarpal is palpable as which surface landmark?","choices":["The base of the thumbnail","A knuckle of the clenched fist","The crease line at the wrist","The very tip of the finger"],"answer":1,"exp":"The heads of the metacarpals form the knuckles that become prominent when the fist is clenched. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 2"},{"q":"Each adult hip (coxal) bone is formed by the fusion of which three bones?","choices":["Ilium, sacrum, and pubis","Ischium, pubis, and coccyx","Ilium, ischium, and pubis","Ilium, ischium, and sacrum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each coxal (hip) bone forms from the fusion of the ilium, ischium, and pubis, which meet at the acetabulum. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which cartilaginous joint unites the two pubic bones anteriorly?","choices":["The sacroiliac joint","The acetabular joint","The obturator joint","The pubic symphysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The two pubic bones meet anteriorly at the pubic symphysis, a cartilaginous joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which large, weight-bearing prominence of the ischium supports the body when sitting?","choices":["The ischial tuberosity","The ischial spine","The iliac crest","The pubic tubercle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ischial tuberosity is the stout inferior prominence of the ischium that bears the body's weight during sitting. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The acetabulum serves as the socket for which articulation?","choices":["The knee joint with the patella","The hip joint with the head of the femur","The sacroiliac joint with the sacrum","The shoulder joint with the humerus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The acetabulum is the deep lateral socket of the hip bone that receives the head of the femur to form the hip joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"How does the subpubic (pubic) angle typically compare between the female and male pelvis?","choices":["Wider in the male than in the female","Identical in both sexes","Wider in the female than in the male","Absent in the female"],"answer":2,"exp":"The subpubic angle is characteristically wider (greater than about 90 degrees) in the female pelvis and narrower in the male pelvis, an adaptation for childbirth. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"Which small pit on the head of the femur anchors the ligament of the head (ligamentum teres)?","choices":["The greater trochanter","The intercondylar fossa","The linea aspera","The fovea capitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"The fovea capitis is the small central pit on the femoral head that anchors the ligament of the head of the femur. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which prominent roughened ridge runs longitudinally along the posterior shaft of the femur?","choices":["The linea aspera","The intertrochanteric line","The soleal line","The pectineal line"],"answer":0,"exp":"The linea aspera is the prominent longitudinal ridge on the posterior surface of the femoral shaft that serves as a major muscle-attachment site. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 0"},{"q":"The patella is classified as what kind of bone?","choices":["A short carpal bone","The largest sesamoid bone","A long bone of the leg","An irregular bone of the pelvis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The patella is the largest sesamoid bone, embedded within the tendon of the quadriceps femoris as it crosses the knee. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 1"},{"q":"Which small anterior pubic projection sits just lateral to the pubic symphysis and serves as a ligament attachment?","choices":["The ischial tuberosity","The greater trochanter","The anterior iliac crest","The pubic tubercle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pubic tubercle is a small forward-projecting bump of the pubis, lateral to the pubic symphysis, that anchors the inguinal ligament. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"}]};
